## 2. Schéma Relationnel de la Base de Données  

| Table | PK | Colonnes principales | FK | Relation |
|-------|----|----------------------|----|----------|
| **users** | `id` (UUID) | email, password_hash, first_name, last_name, role | – | 1‑N → rentals |
| **cars** | `id` (UUID) | make, model, year, license_plate, status, daily_rate | – | 1‑N → rentals |
| **locations** | `id` (UUID) | city, address, zip_code | – | 1‑N → rentals (pickup_location_id, dropoff_location_id) |
| **rentals** | `id` (UUID) | start_date, end_date, total_price, status | `user_id` → users.id (N‑1) <br> `car_id` → cars.id (N‑1) <br> `pickup_location_id` → locations.id (N‑1) <br> `dropoff_location_id` → locations.id (N‑1) | N‑1 → users, cars, locations |
| **payments** | `id` (UUID) | amount, payment_date, method, status | `rental_id` → rentals.id (N‑1) | N‑1 → rentals |
| **car_images** | `id` (UUID) | url, description | `car_id` → cars.id (N‑1) | N‑1 → cars |
| **audit_logs** | `id` (UUID) | action, timestamp, performed_by | `user_id` → users.id (N‑1) | N‑1 → users |

*Contraintes d’unicité* : `users.email`, `cars.license_plate`.  
*Index* : `rentals.status`, `payments.status`.

